<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Łódź</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lodz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Łódź is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> that grew from a small town into a great textile centre in the 19th century, after the government of Congress Poland promoted cloth-making there from the 1820s. Its factories made it the Manchester of Poland and drew Polish, Jewish and German workers. Under Nazi occupation it held the Łódź Ghetto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lodz.jpg" alt="Łódź" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michał Tomczak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_Zes%C5%82ania_Ducha_%C5%9Awi%C4%99tego.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
